  "introduction": {
    "overview": "Mental calculation is a set of habits more than formulas: work left to right so that the largest part of the answer is fixed first, round a number to a friendly value and adjust, split a multiplication into easy parts, and use doubling and halving to make a product easy. These habits cut the time and the writing needed for arithmetic, and they make estimation natural.",
    "placementRelevance": "Online tests often disable or limit rough work, so mental methods save time and reduce errors.",
    "realWorldContext": "Everyday mental arithmetic uses these same habits."
  },
